// error
// #4334: `for x of m` binds the whole `(K, V)` pair, so `x` has a REAL type
// and a mistyped use of it is a checker error. This is the half of #4334 a
// `// run` golden cannot pin: lowering re-derives the pair type from the map
// instance (`lowerForOfBindMap`, lowerctrl.bit) and rebinds the checker name
// before it lowers the body, so a program that only USES the binder correctly
// runs correctly even when `elementType`'s Map branch (checkmatch.bit) answers
// 0 — measured, not assumed. What 0 loses is the diagnostic: an untyped binder
// is assignable both ways under the invalid-cascade contract
// (validate.bit), so `let bad: bool = x` passes silently.
//
// E0088 used to reject this program's loop header outright. It is retired
// (epic #4332's owner ruling: `of` yields the value, and a map's value is the
// pair), so the header below must be ACCEPTED and only the body's type
// mismatch reported.
fn main() {
  let m = map<string, int>{ "ab": 5 }
  for x of m {
    let bad: bool = x
    println("${bad}")
  }
}
